b/id #edb/ref 17592186420055} {:answer/id #uuid "f42f46a3-3d86-5883-8166-5a8c5660c5bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ doesn't divide $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f42f46a3-3d86-5883-8166-5a8c5660c5bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ doesn't divide $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420057} :answer-field/id #uuid "f06f4411-1ba0-55d0-b599-72a83087bfcd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420068} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2faf54bf-f0f4-5f6a-ba0d-fb8c4f36a6dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "for any" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420034} {:answer/id #uuid "b39b7cd8-90e9-51e8-b9f4-44a803b1ea54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "for some" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2faf54bf-f0f4-5f6a-ba0d-fb8c4f36a6dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "for any" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420034} :answer-field/id #uuid "f07d0427-ca84-53ef-b241-840f24e28d37" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8eb0e9a4-7a3a-5999-9897-bbedb74b2220" :question/math-academy-id "q-221920" :question/problem "Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe notation $5∤6$ means {{field-1}}. It is equivalent to the statement that {{field-2}}{{field-3}} $k,$ we have {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The notation $a∤b$ means $a$ does not divides $b.$ So, $5∤6$ means $5\\text{ doesn't divide }6.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ does not divide $b$ means that for any integer $k,$ we have that \n\n$$\nb\\ne ak.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $5∤6$ is equivalent to the statement that $\\text{for any}$ $\\text{integer}$ $k,$ we have $6\\ne 5k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "559e653a-2ab9-54a8-84e3-02bf106a8f47" :knowledge-point/title "Inferring Divisibility in Algebraic Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17d5b6db-f2d7-57e8-a2a5-1d92b46e0512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420031} {:answer/id #uuid "60887104-df4a-53c7-a034-7138745e6c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420036} {:answer/id #uuid "75e38757-7df1-5cf4-b8a8-25b31d2f0338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420038} {:answer/id #uuid "afaa078f-f8ba-5d53-96fa-429e2e0695f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420048} {:answer/id #uuid "e34f1333-ff13-539f-98a3-6487bacbdcd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e34f1333-ff13-539f-98a3-6487bacbdcd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420056} :answer-field/id #uuid "96a743b7-fda2-51fe-bfdd-1a87d90bbfe6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48fbd1ea-16f2-572a-98a1-c1983af51b24" :question/math-academy-id "q-221923" :question/problem "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\n- $3∣(14x+21y)$\n- $7∣(14x+21y)$\n- $4∣(14x+21y)$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0,$ we have $14(1)+21(0)=14,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n14x+21y=7(2x+3y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $2x+3y$ must be an integer, too. So, $7∣(14x+21y).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0,$ we have $14(1)+21(0)=14,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "559e653a-2ab9-54a8-84e3-02bf106a8f47" :knowledge-point/title "Inferring Divisibility in Algebraic Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1da65dc0-01b4-5b68-9fb5-2b24392ab3e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420032} {:answer/id #uuid "7d0c5418-7d81-5d69-80db-f47bba1fe715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420040} {:answer/id #uuid "81fb3c0b-8063-5f9d-b742-0b572de22bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420042} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5ebb79-57bf-59e0-84ab-0b8b6661babd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420044} {:answer/id #uuid "cfef5e04-f733-5d5c-8a6b-71ad9b16f171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfef5e04-f733-5d5c-8a6b-71ad9b16f171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420054} :answer-field/id #uuid "39c81008-53e2-57d5-9021-9e6bc5fda77a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63936aa1-e722-5014-a82a-6fe76f4b851d" :question/math-academy-id "q-221929" :question/problem "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\n- $2$ divides $(6x-18y)$\n- $3$ divides $(6x-18y)$\n- $5$ divides $(6x-18y)$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n6x-18y=2(3x-9y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $3x-9y$ must be an integer, too. So, $2$ divides $(6x-18y).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n6x-18y=3(2x-6y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $2x-6y$ must be an integer, too. So, $3$ divides $(6x-18y).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, if $x=2$ and $y=1,$ we have $6(2)-18(1)=-6,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
